  {
   "h": "Using numbers",
   "body": [
    "Age: *J'ai trente-quatre ans.* Prices: *Ça coûte douze dollars cinquante* (12,50 $). In Quebec, the dollar sign goes after the number and the decimal mark is a comma: *12,50 $*.",
    "Phone numbers are read in groups: area code, then three digits, then two pairs or four digits: *514 555-0199* → *cinq cent quatorze, cinq cinq cinq, zéro un, quatre-vingt-dix-neuf* (many Quebecers simply say each digit)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Quebec: same numbers, local money words",
   "body": [
    "Quebec uses exactly the same numbers as France (*soixante-dix, quatre-vingts, quatre-vingt-dix*), not the Belgian or Swiss *septante, nonante*. One accent detail: you will often hear the t of *vingt* pronounced on its own: [vint].",
    "Money talk is where Quebec stands out: a dollar is informally *une piasse* (from 'piastre'), a cent is *une cenne*, and a quarter is *un trente sous*. *Ça coûte cinq piasses* = it costs five bucks."
   ]
  }
 ],
